export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"partcraft","addon":"advanced-sim","name":"Advanced simulation","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"advanced-sim-start","scope":"addon:advanced-sim","title":"Advanced simulation: getting started","summary":"What the Advanced simulation add-on does in PartCraft, how to switch it on and how to run your first nonlinear study.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["advanced simulation","nonlinear","plasticity","yield","contact","calculix","add-on","simulation"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-nonlinear-study","text":"Your first nonlinear study","level":2},{"id":"what-the-material-does","text":"What the material does","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdvanced simulation goes past PartCraft&#039;s linear static study: material that yields, separate pieces of a part that press on each other, and files for CalculiX, a separate analysis program you install and run yourself. It is an add-on: it comes with PartCraft, is free, and is off until you switch it on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdvanced simulation\u003C/span\u003E. Its tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdvanced sim\u003C/span\u003E, is then in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works on the part&#039;s own Simulation study: the material, the supports and the loads you set in the Simulation workspace stay as they are. The tab changes the study&#039;s type, its load steps and its contact pairs, runs it, and shows the result. Every change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an analysis aid for an engineer, not a design approval. Check the numbers against a hand calculation or another program before you rely on them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-nonlinear-study\"\u003EYour first nonlinear study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up a static study in the Simulation workspace first: pick a material that has a yield stress, add supports and loads on faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdvanced sim\u003C/span\u003E tab and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENonlinear\u003C/span\u003E. The study becomes a nonlinear study; its supports, loads and material are kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad steps\u003C/span\u003E (10 is a good start) and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope after yield (MPa)\u003C/span\u003E. Leave the slope empty for 1 % of the material&#039;s E. A slope of 0 is perfectly plastic: past the load the part can carry, the steps stop converging and the result says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun study\u003C/span\u003E. The result lists the load carried, the largest displacement and von Mises stress, the largest plastic strain, and a table of the load steps (how many Newton iterations each took and how many points yielded).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E (von Mises stress, plastic strain or displacement) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow beside the part\u003C/span\u003E. The deformed shape is drawn beside the part in eight colour bands, blue for the lowest values and red for the highest, with the shape scaled so you can see it (the note says by how much). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the plot\u003C/span\u003E takes it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plot is made of display bodies: it has no mass, is not part of the solid and is not exported. For the full plot with probes, solve the same study in the Simulation workspace: it is the same study.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-material-does\"\u003EWhat the material does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe material is bilinear: straight up to its yield stress with slope E, then straight on with the slope after yield. Plastic flow follows von Mises (J2) with isotropic hardening, at small strains. That is the model the results come from; it is not a curve fitted to a test.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Advanced simulation goes past PartCraft's linear static study: material that yields, separate pieces of a part that press on each other, and files for CalculiX, a separate analysis program you install and run yourself. It is an add-on: it comes with PartCraft, is free, and is off until you switch it on in Settings › General › Add-ons › Advanced simulation. Its tab, Advanced sim, is then in the right panel. It works on the part's own Simulation study: the material, the supports and the loads you set in the Simulation workspace stay as they are. The tab changes the study's type, its load steps and its contact pairs, runs it, and shows the result. Every change is one undo step. It is an analysis aid for an engineer, not a design approval. Check the numbers against a hand calculation or another program before you rely on them. Your first nonlinear study Set up a static study in the Simulation workspace first: pick a material that has a yield stress, add supports and loads on faces. Open the Advanced sim tab and press Nonlinear. The study becomes a nonlinear study; its supports, loads and material are kept. Set Load steps (10 is a good start) and the Slope after yield (MPa). Leave the slope empty for 1 % of the material's E. A slope of 0 is perfectly plastic: past the load the part can carry, the steps stop converging and the result says so. Press Run study. The result lists the load carried, the largest displacement and von Mises stress, the largest plastic strain, and a table of the load steps (how many Newton iterations each took and how many points yielded). Pick a Plot (von Mises stress, plastic strain or displacement) and press Show beside the part. The deformed shape is drawn beside the part in eight colour bands, blue for the lowest values and red for the highest, with the shape scaled so you can see it (the note says by how much). Remove the plot takes it away. The plot is made of display bodies: it has no mass, is not part of the solid and is not exported. For the full plot with probes, solve the same study in the Simulation workspace: it is the same study. What the material does The material is bilinear: straight up to its yield stress with slope E, then straight on with the slope after yield. Plastic flow follows von Mises (J2) with isotropic hardening, at small strains. That is the model the results come from; it is not a curve fitted to a test."},{"slug":"advanced-sim-contact","scope":"addon:advanced-sim","title":"Advanced simulation: contact between pieces","summary":"Run a study in which separate pieces of a part press on each other, find the touching faces and read the contact force and penetration.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["contact","frictionless","bonded","penalty","touching faces","pieces","gap","penetration"],"headings":[{"id":"when-to-use-it","text":"When to use it","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"reading-the-result","text":"Reading the result","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"when-to-use-it\"\u003EWhen to use it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part can hold several separate pieces: for example two rectangles in one sketch, extruded, are two bars. A contact study lets the load go from one piece to the other through the faces where they meet, instead of treating them as one solid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the material, supports and loads in the Simulation workspace. Each piece must be held well enough that it cannot fly away sideways: contact pushes only across the joint.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdvanced sim\u003C/span\u003E tab press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContact\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind touching faces\u003C/span\u003E. It lists flat faces of different pieces that face each other no more than the distance in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELook for faces up to (mm) apart\u003C/span\u003E (0.5 mm to start). Check the list, then press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor each pair choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrictionless\u003C/span\u003E (the faces may open and slide, they cannot pass through each other) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBonded\u003C/span\u003E (glued). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPenalty factor\u003C/span\u003E sets how stiff the contact is (20 to start).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun study\u003C/span\u003E. The result adds the number of points touching, the contact force, and the penetration: how far the pieces sank into each other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurved faces, or faces the finder does not see, can be paired by hand in the Simulation workspace: add a contact pair there and pick its two sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reading-the-result\"\u003EReading the result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EContact is frictionless or bonded; results of a penalty method carry the stated penetration; use CalculiX for friction and large sliding.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe penalty method lets the pieces sink a little into each other. When the penetration is more than 1 % of the element size the result says so and a Problem appears: raise the pair&#039;s penalty factor and run again. A bonded joint is a little soft for the same reason; raise the factor, not the mesh.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESide A of a pair is the side that presses; side B the side it presses on. Side B should not have much finer elements than side A. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap sides\u003C/span\u003E turns a pair round.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"When to use it A part can hold several separate pieces: for example two rectangles in one sketch, extruded, are two bars. A contact study lets the load go from one piece to the other through the faces where they meet, instead of treating them as one solid. Steps Set the material, supports and loads in the Simulation workspace. Each piece must be held well enough that it cannot fly away sideways: contact pushes only across the joint. In the Advanced sim tab press Contact. Press Find touching faces. It lists flat faces of different pieces that face each other no more than the distance in Look for faces up to (mm) apart (0.5 mm to start). Check the list, then press Add. For each pair choose Frictionless (the faces may open and slide, they cannot pass through each other) or Bonded (glued). Penalty factor sets how stiff the contact is (20 to start). Press Run study. The result adds the number of points touching, the contact force, and the penetration: how far the pieces sank into each other. Curved faces, or faces the finder does not see, can be paired by hand in the Simulation workspace: add a contact pair there and pick its two sides. Reading the result Contact is frictionless or bonded; results of a penalty method carry the stated penetration; use CalculiX for friction and large sliding. The penalty method lets the pieces sink a little into each other. When the penetration is more than 1 % of the element size the result says so and a Problem appears: raise the pair's penalty factor and run again. A bonded joint is a little soft for the same reason; raise the factor, not the mesh. Side A of a pair is the side that presses; side B the side it presses on. Side B should not have much finer elements than side A. Swap sides turns a pair round."},{"slug":"advanced-sim-calculix","scope":"addon:advanced-sim","title":"Advanced simulation: CalculiX files","summary":"Save a CalculiX input deck of the study, run it in CalculiX yourself, and read its results back. The decks have never been run in CalculiX.","section":"reference","order":3,"keywords":["calculix","ccx","inp","frd","input deck","export","results","check"],"headings":[{"id":"not-checked-in-calculix-yet","text":"Not checked in CalculiX yet","level":2},{"id":"save-a-deck","text":"Save a deck","level":2},{"id":"read-results-back","text":"Read results back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-checked-in-calculix-yet\"\u003ENot checked in CalculiX yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CalculiX decks are written to CalculiX&#039;s manual and have never been run in CalculiX. Every deck starts with a comment that says so. Expect to fix the first deck by hand; if CalculiX refuses one, send its .log to CadCraft so the writer can be fixed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECalculiX is a separate program you install and run yourself. Nothing in PartCraft runs it, ships it or links to it: PartCraft writes a text file for it and reads a text file from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-deck\"\u003ESave a deck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a nonlinear or contact study set up, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave CalculiX deck (.inp)\u003C/span\u003E. The deck has the same mesh as the study (quadratic tetrahedra, C3D10), the material (elastic, density, and the yield stress with the slope after yield as a plastic curve), the supports, the loads as nodal forces, each contact pair as surfaces with a linear penalty (frictionless) or a tie (bonded), and one static step with the load steps as increments. Units are mm, N, MPa and tonnes. It asks CalculiX for displacements, stresses, strains and plastic strain.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-results-back\"\u003ERead results back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERun the deck in CalculiX. Then, without changing the part or the study, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead CalculiX results (.frd)\u003C/span\u003E and pick the .frd file CalculiX wrote. The result shows the displacement (and stresses, when the file has them) on the same mesh; the plots work as for PartCraft&#039;s own result. A file from another mesh is refused: write the deck again and rerun CalculiX.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Not checked in CalculiX yet The CalculiX decks are written to CalculiX's manual and have never been run in CalculiX. Every deck starts with a comment that says so. Expect to fix the first deck by hand; if CalculiX refuses one, send its .log to CadCraft so the writer can be fixed. CalculiX is a separate program you install and run yourself. Nothing in PartCraft runs it, ships it or links to it: PartCraft writes a text file for it and reads a text file from it. Save a deck With a nonlinear or contact study set up, press Save CalculiX deck (.inp). The deck has the same mesh as the study (quadratic tetrahedra, C3D10), the material (elastic, density, and the yield stress with the slope after yield as a plastic curve), the supports, the loads as nodal forces, each contact pair as surfaces with a linear penalty (frictionless) or a tie (bonded), and one static step with the load steps as increments. Units are mm, N, MPa and tonnes. It asks CalculiX for displacements, stresses, strains and plastic strain. Read results back Run the deck in CalculiX. Then, without changing the part or the study, press Read CalculiX results (.frd) and pick the .frd file CalculiX wrote. The result shows the displacement (and stresses, when the file has them) on the same mesh; the plots work as for PartCraft's own result. A file from another mesh is refused: write the deck again and rerun CalculiX."},{"slug":"advanced-sim-limits","scope":"addon:advanced-sim","title":"Advanced simulation: limits and troubleshooting","summary":"What the nonlinear and contact studies do not solve, and what to do when a study stops or the pieces sink into each other.","section":"trouble","order":4,"keywords":["limits","not converged","penetration","friction","large deformation","slow","mesh"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-does-not-solve","text":"What it does not solve","level":2},{"id":"a-step-did-not-converge","text":"A step did not converge","level":2},{"id":"the-pieces-sink-into-each-other","text":"The pieces sink into each other","level":2},{"id":"slow-studies","text":"Slow studies","level":2},{"id":"find-touching-faces-finds-nothing","text":"Find touching faces finds nothing","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-solve\"\u003EWhat it does not solve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELarge rotations and large strains: the studies are small-strain and small-displacement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFriction, preload and large sliding: contact is frictionless or bonded only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther plasticity models: only the bilinear material (yield stress and one slope after it), isotropic hardening, no unloading history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EContact between parts of an assembly: the pieces must be in one part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-step-did-not-converge\"\u003EA step did not converge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result says at what share of the load it stopped, and a Problem says the same. Common causes: the load is above what the part can carry with a perfectly plastic material (slope 0), too few load steps, or a piece that is not held. Use more load steps, a slope after yield above 0, or check the supports.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pieces-sink-into-each-other\"\u003EThe pieces sink into each other\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPenetration over 1 % of the element size is a Problem. Raise the pair&#039;s penalty factor (10 times is a good step) and run again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"slow-studies\"\u003ESlow studies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach load step is a full solve, and contact adds passes that pair the faces again. Keep the element size as large as the question allows. A large mesh is a job for a workstation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-touching-faces-finds-nothing\"\u003EFind touching faces finds nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt looks only at flat faces of different pieces that face each other within the distance you give. Raise the distance, or pair curved faces by hand in the Simulation workspace.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it does not solve Large rotations and large strains: the studies are small-strain and small-displacement. Friction, preload and large sliding: contact is frictionless or bonded only. Other plasticity models: only the bilinear material (yield stress and one slope after it), isotropic hardening, no unloading history. Contact between parts of an assembly: the pieces must be in one part. A step did not converge The result says at what share of the load it stopped, and a Problem says the same. Common causes: the load is above what the part can carry with a perfectly plastic material (slope 0), too few load steps, or a piece that is not held. Use more load steps, a slope after yield above 0, or check the supports. The pieces sink into each other Penetration over 1 % of the element size is a Problem. Raise the pair's penalty factor (10 times is a good step) and run again. Slow studies Each load step is a full solve, and contact adds passes that pair the faces again. Keep the element size as large as the question allows. A large mesh is a job for a workstation. Find touching faces finds nothing It looks only at flat faces of different pieces that face each other within the distance you give. Raise the distance, or pair curved faces by hand in the Simulation workspace."}]};
